TITLE:: VCFQ
summary:: Serge-Style Variable-Q Ping Resonator and Multimode VCF
categories:: UGens>Filters
related:: Guides/Filtra, Classes/Wasp, Classes/Steiner, Classes/MS20TPT

DESCRIPTION::
VCFQ is a faithful digital model of the iconic Serge Modular VCFQ (Variable-Q Filter) designed by Serge Tcherepnin.

The VCFQ is famous in modular synthesis for its twin capabilities:
LIST::
## Serving as a high-character multimode voltage-controlled filter with wide, dynamic resonance sweeps.
## Operating as an acoustic percussion resonator and "ping" generator, excited by trigger or gate pulses at its dedicated trigger input without requiring any continuous audio input.
::

Under the hood, VCFQ uses a 2nd-order Topology-Preserving Transform (TPT) State-Variable Filter (SVF) engine equipped with an Automatic Gain Control (AGC) diode feedback saturation loop. When excited by an incoming pulse at the code::trig:: port, the filter rings at its tuned cutoff frequency, producing expressive acoustic bells, wooden strikes, kick drums, and damped marimba tones depending on code::freq:: and code::q::.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar

ARGUMENT:: in
Audio-rate input signal to filter. Set to code::DC.ar(0):: when using pure ping percussion synthesis.

ARGUMENT:: trig
Trigger or impulse excitation input (control or audio rate). A positive edge transition (<= 0 to > 0) injects an impulse into the resonant state, causing the filter to "ping" and ring.

ARGUMENT:: freq
Cutoff or ringing frequency in Hz (control or audio rate). Range [10 Hz, Nyquist * 0.49].

ARGUMENT:: q
Resonance quality factor (range [0.5, 50.0]). At high Q, the filter produces prolonged ringing acoustic tails.

ARGUMENT:: drive
Nonlinear feedback and diode drive gain (range [0.1, 20.0], default 1.0). Higher values engage smooth AGC limiting, warming the decay and preventing self-oscillation blowup.

ARGUMENT:: mode
Filter mode selection (integer, init-only):
TABLE::
## Mode || Type
## 0 || Low-Pass (LP)
## 1 || Band-Pass (BP - standard for acoustic ping ringing)
## 2 || High-Pass (HP)
## 3 || Notch (Band-Reject)
::

ARGUMENT:: mul
Output scale factor.

ARGUMENT:: add
Output offset.

returns:: An audio-rate filtered signal.

EXAMPLES::

CODE::
s.boot;

// 1. Classic Serge Trigger Ping Percussion (Zero Audio Input)
(
x = {
    var trig = Impulse.kr(4);
    var pitchSeq = Demand.kr(trig, 0, Dseq([220, 330, 440, 660, 550, 880, 440, 330], inf));
    var qSeq = Demand.kr(trig, 0, Dseq([10.0, 25.0, 40.0, 15.0], inf));
    var ping = VCFQ.ar(DC.ar(0), trig: trig, freq: pitchSeq, q: qSeq, drive: 1.5, mode: 1);
    ping * 0.4 ! 2;
}.play;
)

// 2. Multimode Low-Pass with Dynamic Q Resonance Sweeps
(
x = {
    var saw = Saw.ar([110, 110.5]) * 0.3;
    var cut = SinOsc.kr(0.3).exprange(200, 4500);
    var q = LFNoise2.kr(1).range(2.0, 30.0);
    var sig = VCFQ.ar(saw, trig: 0, freq: cut, q: q, drive: 2.0, mode: 0);
    sig * 0.5 ! 2;
}.play;
)

// 3. Audio-Rate FM Ping Bell Synthesis
(
x = {
    var trig = Dust.kr(3);
    var fmMod = SinOsc.ar(140).exprange(300, 1800);
    var ping = VCFQ.ar(DC.ar(0), trig: trig, freq: fmMod, q: 35.0, drive: 3.0, mode: 1);
    ping * 0.4 ! 2;
}.play;
)
::
